Object storage is a storage system designed to store large amounts of unstructured data as objects, such as:
- Images
- Videos
- Audio
- PDFs/documents
- Backups
- Log/archive files
Instead of storing data as database rows or traditional filesystem blocks, object storage stores each item as an object containing the data, metadata, and an identifier/key.

Simple example
Suppose you're designing Instagram.
Users upload millions of photos and videos.
You generally wouldn't store all those large media files directly inside your relational database.
Instead:
User uploads photo
       ↓
Application Server
       ↓
 Object Storage
       ↓
   Photo stored

The database can store metadata/reference information:
Post Database

post_id:     123
user_id:     101
caption:     "Vacation!"
image_key:   "posts/101/photo123.jpg"

While the actual image is stored in object storage:
Object Storage

posts/101/photo123.jpg
        ↓
Actual image bytes

Amazon S3
Amazon S3 stands for Amazon Simple Storage Service.
It is AWS's object storage service.
In S3, objects are stored inside buckets.
Conceptually:
Amazon S3

Bucket: instagram-media
│
├── images/
│     ├── image101.jpg
│     ├── image102.jpg
│     └── image103.jpg
│
├── videos/
│     ├── video201.mp4
│     └── video202.mp4
│
└── documents/
      └── file301.pdf

Technically, S3 uses object keys such as images/image101.jpg; the / characters make the keys look folder-like.
An S3 object contains
Conceptually:
Object
├── Data
│     └── image.jpg
│
├── Key
│     └── images/image101.jpg
│
└── Metadata
      ├── Content-Type: image/jpeg
      └── other metadata

Why use object storage?
Object storage such as S3 is designed for:
Massive scalability — can hold huge numbers of objects.
Durability — designed to keep stored data reliably.
Large files — good for images, videos, backups, documents, etc.
Metadata — objects can have associated metadata.
Cost efficiency — often more economical than keeping large media blobs in your primary application database.
HLD example: YouTube
Imagine designing YouTube:
User
 │
 │ Upload video
 ▼
API Server
 │
 ├──────────────→ Database
 │                 │
 │                 └─ title
 │                    userId
 │                    videoId
 │                    object key
 │
 └──────────────→ Object Storage (S3)
                    │
                    └─ actual video

Then for delivering videos globally:
S3 / Object Storage
        ↓
       CDN
        ↓
      Users

This Object Storage + CDN combination is extremely common in system-design discussions.
Object Storage vs Database
Database	Object Storage
Structured/queryable application data	Large unstructured objects
Users, orders, payments	Images, videos, PDFs
Complex queries/indexes	Access primarily by object key
Frequent record updates	Often whole-object-oriented access
PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB	Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Blob Storage


So don't think:
S3 replaces your database. ❌

They usually work together.
Database
→ "What is this video and who owns it?"

Object Storage
→ "Give me the actual video file."

Interview definition
Object storage stores unstructured data such as images, videos, and documents as objects identified by unique keys and associated metadata. Amazon S3 is a widely used object-storage service.

For HLD, the key connection to remember is:
Large static/media files → Object Storage → CDN → Users.